module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjSaturation.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjSaturation.IdealOfClosedSubscheme

/-!
# The ideal of a closed subscheme of `Proj`, on a chart: the reverse inclusion

Let `S = ⨁_{n ≥ 0} 𝒮 n` be a graded ring, let `X = Proj 𝒮`, let `f ∈ 𝒮 d` for some `d > 0`, and
let `i : Z ⟶ X` be a closed immersion. The standard open `D₊(f)` is affine with ring of sections
the degree-zero localization `HomogeneousLocalization.Away 𝒮 f`, whose elements are the fractions
`b / f ^ n` with `b` homogeneous of degree `n · d`.

`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjChartKernel/Basic.lean` proves one inclusion of the chart
comparison: if `b` lies in `idealOfClosedSubscheme 𝒮 i` then `b / f ^ n` lies in
`Ker(i.app D₊(f))`. This file proves the **reverse** inclusion, and hence the chart-level
equality: every element of `Ker(i.app D₊(f))` is `c / f ^ m` for some homogeneous `c` *in the
ideal of `Z`*.

The argument is the one in the source. An element of the chart ring is `b / f ^ n` for some
homogeneous `b`; the claim is that `f ^ N b` lies in `I(Z)` for `N` large. Membership in `I(Z)` is
chartwise, so fix a second element `h` of the *same* degree `d` and work over `D₊(h)`. Over the
intersection `D₊(hf)` the two chart functions `b / h ^ n` and `b / f ^ n` differ by a unit, so
`b / h ^ n` restricts into `Ker(i.app D₊(hf))`. Now `Ker(i.app ·)` *localizes*: the kernel over
`D₊(hf)` is the ideal generated by the kernel over `D₊(h)`, because an ideal sheaf's ideals are
related by `Scheme.IdealSheafData.map_ideal`. And `Away 𝒮 (hf)` is the localization of `Away 𝒮 h`
at `f ^ d / h ^ d` (Mathlib's `HomogeneousLocalization.Away.isLocalization_mul`). An element whose
image in a localization lies in the extension of an ideal is carried into that ideal by some power
of the localizing element, so `(f ^ d / h ^ d) ^ k (b / h ^ n) = f ^ (k d) b / h ^ (k d + n)` lies
in `Ker(i.app D₊(h))` for some `k` depending on `h`.

A *finite* family of elements of one common degree `d` is then enough to finish: the maximum of
those exponents serves for every chart at once, and the chartwise criterion
`mem_idealOfClosedSubscheme_of_forall_chartFraction_mem_ker` puts `f ^ N b` in `I(Z)`. Such a
family exists as soon as `Proj 𝒮` is quasi-compact
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Proj.exists_common_degree_finite_basicOpen_cover`), which is Tag 0801's only
hypothesis; over a Noetherian ring generated in degree one one may take `d = 1` instead, which is
`exists_finite_degreeOne_cover`.

That last paragraph is the **only** use of the Noetherian hypothesis in this file, and it uses it
only to produce the finite family.  Where such a family is at hand — for a polynomial ring it is
the list of variables — no Noetherian hypothesis is needed, and
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjQuotientFiniteCover/Basic.lean` restates the chain with the
family supplied instead.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.chartFraction_eq_of_cross`: two chart functions are
  equal exactly when the cross-multiplied numerators agree. This is the general form of
  `chartFraction_pow_mul_left`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.chartFraction_mul` and `chartFraction_pow`: the chart
  function of a product is the product of the chart functions.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.exists_chartFraction_eq`: every section over `D₊(f)`
  is a chart function.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.chartFraction_restrict`: restricting a chart function
  to a smaller standard open multiplies numerator and denominator.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.exists_pow_mul_chartFraction_mem_ker`: **the
  localization step** — one chart's kernel membership gives, after multiplication by a power of
  that chart's element, kernel membership on any other chart of the *same* degree.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.exists_finite_degreeOne_cover`: over a Noetherian
  ring generated in degree one, finitely many degree-one standard opens cover `Proj 𝒮`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.exists_pow_mul_mem_idealOfClosedSubscheme`: given a
  finite cover by standard opens of one common degree, one exponent works for every chart, so
  `f ^ N b` lies in `I(Z)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.exists_mem_idealOfClosedSubscheme_chartFraction_eq`:
  **the reverse inclusion**.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.mem_ker_app_basicOpen_iff`: **the chart-level
  equality**, both inclusions together, and its projective-space form
  `projectiveSpace_mem_ker_app_basicOpen_iff`.

## Implementation notes

Two spellings of `awayToSection` are in play and the distinction is load-bearing for `rw`.
`AlgebraicGeometry.Proj.awayToSection` (`Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjectiveSpectrum/Basic.lean`)
is by definition `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.awayToSection`, which is what
`chartFraction` is built from, and inside this namespace the bare `Proj.awayToSection` resolves to
the second. They are definitionally equal, so a term-mode `exact` crosses between them freely —
`Function.Bijective` of one is proved from `basicOpenIsoAway` of the other with no bridge — but
`rw` matches syntactically, so the restriction square and the ideal computation that rewrite with
it are written throughout in Mathlib's spelling, and only the final `rfl` bridges name
`chartFraction`.

The localization is taken in the chart rings rather than in the rings of sections, so that
Mathlib's `HomogeneousLocalization.Away.isLocalization_mul` applies on the nose: its algebra
instance is induced by `HomogeneousLocalization.awayMap`, which is the same map that Mathlib's
`AlgebraicGeometry.Proj.awayMap_awayToSection` squares with restriction. Transporting the kernel
ideals across `basicOpenIsoAway` instead needs only one inclusion, obtained from
`Ideal.map_comap_of_surjective` and `Ideal.comap_map_of_surjective` with the isomorphism's
injectivity killing the `⊔ ker` term.

Degree indices are kept in the form `k • d` rather than `k * d`, which is the indexing
`chartFraction` uses, and converted by `add_smul`/`smul_add` rather than by a cast. Where an
exponent has to be split, `Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le` introduces the difference as a variable so
that `ring` can see `f ^ (a + M) = f ^ a * f ^ M`; truncated subtraction would defeat it.

Two equations are proved as standalone `have`s rather than inline in the `rw` that consumes them
(`hpowe` and `hcross`). Both relate elements that also occur in the *type* of a neighbouring
homogeneity proof, so a `rw` inside the dependent position cannot build a motive; stated on their
own the equations are about ring elements only, and `rw` then matches them where the dependency
has already been discharged.

No `@[stacks 0801]` attribute is attached. What is proved here is the chart-level comparison of
ideals, in both directions. Tag 0801 asserts more: that every closed subscheme of a relative
`Proj` is the closed subscheme cut out by a graded ideal sheaf, presented as a relative `Proj`
itself. The chart comparison is the computational heart of that statement and not the statement.

**Neither of Tag 0801's non-hypotheses is carried any more.** Tag 0801 assumes only that the
structure morphism be quasi-compact; neither `[IsNoetherianRing S]` nor
`Algebra.adjoin (𝒮 0) (𝒮 1) = ⊤` appears in it, so neither may be inherited by a declaration
registering the tag, and the statements of this file now carry neither. What replaced them:

* the Noetherian hypothesis was spent **only** on making the cover finite, so that the exponent
  `Finset.univ.sup NN` is uniform; the cover is now a hypothesis, asked for as a family over a
  `Finite` index type, and quasi-compactness of `Proj 𝒮` supplies one through
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Proj.exists_common_degree_finite_basicOpen_cover`;
* degree-one generation was spent on covering `Proj 𝒮` by degree-one standard opens and, inside
  `mem_idealOfClosedSubscheme_of_forall_chartFraction_mem_ker`, on the twist-invertibility
  instances; both now come from the covering family itself, the second by
  `algebraicGeometry_isInvertible_twistingSheaf_of_cover`, which needs only a cover by standard
  opens of one common degree `d` and gives `𝒪(k · d)` invertible for every `k`;
* the localization step's degree-one restriction was structural — it forms `h * f` and appeals to
  `HomogeneousLocalization.Away.isLocalization_mul`, so `f` and `h` must have *equal* degree for
  `f ^ d / h ^ d` to be a degree-zero fraction. Equal degrees are what
  `exists_common_degree_finite_basicOpen_cover` produces, by raising each member of a covering
  family of degrees `d k` to the power `D / d k` for `D` a common multiple, which changes no
  standard open since `D₊(f ^ e) = D₊(f)`.

The degree-one specializations are still available to the degree-one consumers of the chart
comparison, through `exists_finite_degreeOne_cover`; they pass `d = 1` and a `Finset` of
degree-one elements spanning the irrelevant ideal.

